	abstract = {In recent years the aluminium industry in the Western world has been operating well below capacity, with cutbacks in production due largely to the depressed aluminium market conditions of 1992 and 1993. Since mid-1995, however, aluminium producers have begun restarting idled capacity. Extensive efforts and preparation are required both to close capacity in an orderly manner and to restart idled capacity. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the costs of shutting capacity, maintaining idled capacity, and restarting potlines.},
